WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
In April 2010, a rust fungus turned up

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.880
on plants at one nursery on the New

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.960
South Wales Central Coast.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.960
Within a few years it had spread the

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.080
length of the east coast, on eucalypts, paperbarks

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.440
and lemon myrtle.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.640
So how does disease damage farm production, and

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.720
why does the bill so often reach far

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.800
past the sick plants and animals?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.360
Start with causes.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.960
For a disease to strike, three things have

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.680
to meet: a pathogen, a host it can

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.920
infect, and the right conditions.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.040
Rust spores, for example, need a film of

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.440
water on the leaf to germinate, so wet

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.000
weather and heavy dew raise the risk.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.480
Crops face the same pathogen groups you met

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.800
earlier: fungi, bacteria, viruses and nematodes.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.800
Farming can tip the odds.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.000
Remember the Cavendish banana: every plant is a

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.800
clone, so they all share one weakness.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.760
A paddock of a single variety is similar.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.560
A pathogen that can infect one plant can

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.280
infect the next, with no resistant plants in

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.960
between to slow it.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.480
Moving plants, soil and machinery carries pathogens to

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.240
new districts too.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.720
So the causes are partly biology and partly

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.080
farming, and the farming part can be changed.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.260
What does disease cost a crop?

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.180
First, yield.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.900
Rust pustules take sugars from the leaf and

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.940
destroy green tissue, so the plant makes less

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.100
food and fills less grain.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.380
Root-knot nematodes swell the roots into galls that

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.020
squeeze the xylem, the tubes that carry water

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.740
up, so the plant wilts even in moist

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.220
soil.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.020
Second, quality: shrivelled grain or marked fruit sells

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.060
at a lower grade.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.820
Third, cost: fungicides, resistant varieties and replanting.

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:56.193
Now a real case.

00:01:56.474 --> 00:01:59.233
Myrtle rust is caused by a fungus native

00:01:59.314 --> 00:02:00.993
to South and Central America.

00:02:01.474 --> 00:02:04.673
It attacks the myrtle family, which includes eucalypts,

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:07.593
paperbarks, tea tree and lemon myrtle.

00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:10.993
Australian myrtles had never met this fungus, so

00:02:11.114 --> 00:02:13.073
many species had little resistance.

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:16.273
Its spores travel on the wind, and infected

00:02:16.354 --> 00:02:19.953
nursery plants carried it long distances, fast.

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:24.126
It has since been found in Tasmania, the

00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:28.686
Northern Territory and, in 2022, northern Western Australia.

00:02:29.327 --> 00:02:31.326
Lemon myrtle and tea tree are grown in

00:02:31.487 --> 00:02:33.966
dense rows of one species, which suits the

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:37.486
fungus, so growers now pay for fungicide, and

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:40.606
untreated plantations can lose much of their crop.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.926
The damage runs past the farm too.

00:02:43.407 --> 00:02:46.686
The native guava, once a common rainforest shrub,

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.446
is now critically endangered.

00:02:49.007 --> 00:02:51.566
And this rust can't be eradicated, so the

00:02:51.647 --> 00:02:53.886
costs don't end after one season.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.140
Animals next.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.060
Foot-and-mouth disease is caused by a virus that

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:05.100
infects cloven-hoofed animals: cattle, sheep, pigs and goats.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.980
It spreads by contact, through the air, and

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.300
on vehicles, boots and feed.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.100
The virus causes blisters in the mouth and

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.900
on the feet, so animals stop eating, go

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.300
lame, and make less milk and meat.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.780
Most adults survive.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.980
These are the direct effects: losses in the

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.660
sick animals themselves.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:28.706
Follow the bill.

00:03:29.347 --> 00:03:32.946
In 2001, foot-and-mouth disease spread through Britain.

00:03:33.427 --> 00:03:36.226
To stop it, over six million animals were

00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:40.066
killed, most of them never infected: whole herds

00:03:40.107 --> 00:03:43.026
on infected and neighbouring farms were culled, and

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:45.906
animals stranded by movement bans, short of feed

00:03:45.987 --> 00:03:48.306
and space, were killed for welfare.

00:03:49.027 --> 00:03:52.066
Export markets closed, and the countryside was shut

00:03:52.107 --> 00:03:52.786
to visitors.

00:03:53.347 --> 00:03:56.306
The total came to about eight billion pounds,

00:03:56.387 --> 00:03:58.706
and tourism lost about as much as farming

00:03:58.787 --> 00:03:59.826
and the food industry.

00:04:00.627 --> 00:04:03.746
These are indirect effects: costs beyond the sick

00:04:03.827 --> 00:04:06.706
animals, from control and closed markets.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:11.533
Australia has been free of foot-and-mouth disease since

00:04:11.654 --> 00:04:14.573
1872, and it sells most of its beef

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.453
overseas, about three-quarters.

00:04:17.574 --> 00:04:20.333
One confirmed case would stop exports to many

00:04:20.574 --> 00:04:22.173
markets straight away.

00:04:22.254 --> 00:04:26.253
In 2022, government economists estimated a large outbreak

00:04:26.574 --> 00:04:29.293
could cost about 80 billion dollars over ten

00:04:29.374 --> 00:04:32.413
years, mostly through lost exports.

00:04:32.494 --> 00:04:35.533
That's why, for an exporter, the indirect effects

00:04:35.694 --> 00:04:37.693
usually outweigh the direct ones.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.460
So how do you assess an effect?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.100
Ask how big it is, how long it

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.660
lasts, and how far it reaches.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.180
Rust in one wheat season costs that harvest.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.580
A pathogen that settles in for good, like

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.220
myrtle rust, changes what can be grown for

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:55.940
years.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.300
And a disease that closes markets reaches far

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:00.820
beyond the farms that had it.

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:05.553
So when you assess a disease, count past

00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:06.753
the farm gate.

00:05:06.794 --> 00:05:08.913
The sick plant or animal is only the

00:05:09.074 --> 00:05:10.113
first cost.

00:05:10.394 --> 00:05:13.553
Control, markets and ecosystems are the rest.

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:17.006
Two exam questions.

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:18.046
First.

00:05:18.687 --> 00:05:21.006
A country that exports most of its beef

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:23.966
has been free of foot-and-mouth disease for decades.

00:05:24.607 --> 00:05:26.846
The disease is confirmed in one herd of

00:05:26.927 --> 00:05:27.806
40 cattle.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:31.566
All 40 are culled, along with 2,000 healthy

00:05:31.647 --> 00:05:34.846
cattle on nearby farms, and three trading partners

00:05:34.927 --> 00:05:36.686
suspend imports of its beef.

00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:40.286
Distinguish between the direct and indirect effects of

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:43.006
this outbreak, and explain which is likely to

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:44.046
cost more.

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:45.646
Pause here to answer.

00:05:56.867 --> 00:06:00.066
Direct effects are losses in the infected herd:

00:06:00.107 --> 00:06:03.506
the 40 sick cattle, producing less, then culled.

00:06:04.147 --> 00:06:07.346
Indirect effects come from control and trade: the

00:06:07.427 --> 00:06:10.226
2,000 healthy cattle culled, and the lost beef

00:06:10.307 --> 00:06:10.946
exports.

00:06:11.587 --> 00:06:14.626
The indirect effects will likely cost far more,

00:06:14.747 --> 00:06:17.026
because the country exports most of its beef.

00:06:17.587 --> 00:06:19.906
Counting the healthy cattle as a direct effect

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:21.666
is the classic mistake.

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:24.226
Second.

00:06:24.787 --> 00:06:27.106
Nearly every farm in a district grows the

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:28.386
same wheat variety.

00:06:28.867 --> 00:06:30.946
A new strain of a rust fungus appears

00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:32.626
that can infect this variety.

00:06:33.187 --> 00:06:35.346
Its spores travel on the wind, and the

00:06:35.427 --> 00:06:37.186
season is warm and wet.

00:06:37.747 --> 00:06:40.066
Yields fall across the whole district.

00:06:40.627 --> 00:06:44.466
Explain one biological cause and one agricultural cause

00:06:44.547 --> 00:06:45.506
of these losses.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:47.506
Pause here to answer.

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:02.493
Biological: the new strain can infect this variety,

00:07:02.574 --> 00:07:04.733
and wind spreads its spores.

00:07:04.774 --> 00:07:06.853
Wet weather helps them germinate.

00:07:07.294 --> 00:07:11.453
Agricultural: every farm grows the same variety, so

00:07:11.774 --> 00:07:14.613
every paddock is equally susceptible and nothing slows

00:07:14.654 --> 00:07:15.733
the spread.

00:07:15.774 --> 00:07:18.653
Blaming the weather alone is the classic mistake:

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:21.693
it helps, but the fungus causes the disease.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.900
So for any farm disease, name the cause,

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.140
then follow the effects from the sick host

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.700
out to markets and ecosystems.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.260
Next, how a plant fights back on its

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.300
own: a banksia against a root pathogen.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.740
Here are the key notes.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.900
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:43.780
to copy them.
